<statement>
<p>
	This program implements the game loop for a player-versus-player (EVE) version of Tic-Tac-Toe.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>state</pf> (1 x 1) number — struct object containing the initial state of an EVE Tic-Tac-Toe game.</p>
			<p><pf>ai_X_level</pf> (1 x 1) number — character indicating which <pf>ai_move</pf> code to use for player X.</p>
			<p><pf>ai_O_level</pf> (1 x 1) number — character indicating which <pf>ai_move</pf> code to use for player O.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>state</pf> (1 x 1) number — struct object containing the final state of an EVE Tic-Tac-Toe game.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>Assume the X player moves first.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>This should be exactly like your previous two</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>game_loop</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>codes, except here, both players will get their selections from one of your ai_move codes. Both</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p><pf>ai_move</pf> codes</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>are determined the same way as in your</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>pve_game_loop</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>code.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

</statement>
